            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Irrevocable, violent, and total judgment or destruction, permanently removing an entity so that it never rises again.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Revelation 18:21\",\"Jeremiah 51:63-64\",\"Matthew 18:6\",\"Mark 9:42\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The overcoming of seemingly impossible obstacles through the power of faith (specifically regarding a mountain).\",\"basis\":[\"Mark 11:23\",\"Matthew 21:21\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The permanent removal, forgetting, and absolute forgiveness of sins.\",\"basis\":[\"Micah 7:19\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Eschatological judgment specifically against the Jerusalem Temple (interpreting 'this mountain' as the Temple Mount being destroyed).\",\"basis\":[\"Mark 11:23\",\"Matthew 21:21\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The motif of casting a heavy stone or millstone into the sea universally signifies permanent, irrecoverable removal. In apocalyptic and prophetic literature, it denotes absolute destruction of enemies like Babylon. In moral teachings, it illustrates severe judgment for causing stumbling. Variations depend on the object cast: mountains represent either obstacles moved by faith or the judged Temple Mount, while sins cast into the depths represent total forgiveness.\"}",
